		array(
			'slug' => 'agentic-ai-vs-automation', 'cpt' => 'post',
			'title' => 'Agentic AI vs traditional automation',
			'meta_title' => 'Agentic AI vs Traditional Automation | Alugence',
			'meta_desc' => 'Agentic AI vs traditional automation compared: how they differ, when to use each, and why AI agents handle work rules-based automation can\'t.',
			'excerpt' => 'Traditional automation (including RPA) follows fixed rules and needs structured, predictable inputs. Agentic AI uses AI to plan, reason and act on unstructured, variable inputs, adapting as it goes. Rules-based automation is best for stable, repetitive tasks; agentic AI is best for complex work involving judgement and exceptions.',
			'faqs' => array(array('What is the difference between agentic AI and automation?','Traditional automation follows fixed rules and needs structured inputs; agentic AI uses AI to plan, reason and act on unstructured, variable inputs, adapting to exceptions. Rules-based automation suits stable, repetitive tasks; agentic AI suits complex work involving judgement.'),array('Is agentic AI better than RPA?','Not better, different. RPA (robotic process automation) is excellent for predictable, rules-based tasks and is often cheaper and simpler. Agentic AI handles variability and exceptions RPA can\'t. Many organisations use both, applying each where it fits.'),array('Can agentic AI replace my existing automation?','Not necessarily — existing rules-based automation may still be the best tool for stable tasks. Agentic AI is most valuable where current automation breaks on unstructured inputs or exceptions. A good approach extends what you have rather than ripping it out.'),array('Which should I choose for my process?','Map the process first: if inputs are structured and the logic is fixed, rules-based automation is likely best; if inputs vary and judgement or exception-handling is needed, agentic AI fits. Often a combination wins. Alugence assesses this before building anything.')),
			'html' => '<nav class="on-this-page" aria-label="On this page"><a href="#difference">The key difference</a> · <a href="#table">Comparison</a> · <a href="#when">When to use each</a> · <a href="#both">Using both together</a> · <a href="#faqs">FAQs</a></nav>
<h2 id="difference">The key difference</h2>
<p>Traditional automation executes predefined rules: <em>if X, do Y.</em> It\'s fast and reliable when inputs are structured and predictable, but it breaks the moment something unexpected appears. Agentic AI reasons about a goal, handles messy inputs, and decides what to do — including how to deal with exceptions. One follows a script; the other thinks through the task.</p>
<h2 id="table">Agentic AI vs traditional automation at a glance</h2>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Traditional automation (RPA/rules)</th><th>Agentic AI</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Inputs</strong></td><td>Structured, predictable</td><td>Unstructured, variable</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Logic</strong></td><td>Fixed rules</td><td>Plans and adapts</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Exceptions</strong></td><td>Breaks or escalates</td><td>Reasons about them</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Setup</strong></td><td>Rigid, explicit</td><td>Goal + guardrails</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Best for</strong></td><td>Stable, repetitive tasks</td><td>Complex, variable tasks</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<h2 id="when">When to use each</h2>
<p>Use <strong>traditional automation</strong> for high-volume, stable, rules-based tasks — moving data between systems, triggering fixed workflows. Use <strong>agentic AI</strong> where inputs vary and judgement is needed — reading free-text emails, handling exceptions, completing multi-step tasks that touch several systems. Read <a href="/guides/what-is-agentic-ai/">What is agentic AI?</a> for more.</p>
<h2 id="both">Using both together</h2>
<p>The best solutions often combine them: rules-based automation for the predictable steps, agentic AI for the parts that need understanding and judgement. Alugence designs <a href="/ai-consultancy/ai-automation/">AI automation</a> that uses each where it fits, rather than forcing everything into one approach.</p>
<h2>Automate the right way</h2>
<p>Alugence combines rules-based automation and AI agents to fit your processes. Explore <a href="/ai-consultancy/ai-automation/">AI automation</a> or <a href="/contact/">book a call</a>.</p>',
		),
		array(
			'slug' => 'ai-adoption-barriers', 'cpt' => 'post',
			'title' => 'AI adoption: overcoming common barriers',
			'meta_title' => 'Overcoming Common AI Adoption Barriers | Alugence',
			'meta_desc' => 'The most common barriers to AI adoption — data, skills, trust, cost and change — and practical ways to overcome each and get AI working.',
			'excerpt' => 'The most common barriers to AI adoption are poor data readiness, skills and confidence gaps, trust and governance concerns, unclear ROI, and resistance to change. Each is manageable: fix data foundations, train people, build governance, start with low-risk pilots that prove value, and lead the change actively rather than mandating it.',
			'faqs' => array(array('What are the biggest barriers to AI adoption?','The biggest barriers are poor data readiness, skills and confidence gaps, trust and governance concerns, unclear ROI, and resistance to change. Notably, most are organisational rather than technical — which means they\'re addressable with the right approach.'),array('How do you overcome resistance to AI in a business?','Involve people early, show how AI helps their own work rather than threatens it, provide practical training, and start with a visible quick win that builds confidence. Leading the change actively — with clear communication and support — works far better than mandating adoption.'),array('Why do AI projects fail to get adopted?','They most often fail on adoption factors — weak data, insufficient training, unclear value, or neglected change management — rather than the technology. Addressing data, skills, governance, ROI and change management is what turns AI investment into real use.'),array('What\'s the first step to successful AI adoption?','Assess your readiness and start with one low-risk, high-value use case that proves value quickly. A visible early win builds the confidence, evidence and momentum needed to overcome the other barriers and scale.')),
			'html' => '<nav class="on-this-page" aria-label="On this page"><a href="#barriers">The barriers</a> · <a href="#overcome">How to overcome them</a> · <a href="#faqs">FAQs</a></nav>
<h2 id="barriers">The five common AI adoption barriers</h2>
<ol><li><strong>Data readiness</strong> — data that\'s siloed, messy or inaccessible.</li><li><strong>Skills and confidence</strong> — teams unsure how to use AI well.</li><li><strong>Trust and governance</strong> — concerns about accuracy, security and compliance.</li><li><strong>Unclear ROI</strong> — no business case to justify investment.</li><li><strong>Change resistance</strong> — people worried about disruption or their roles.</li></ol>
<h2 id="overcome">How to overcome each barrier</h2>
<ul><li><strong>Data:</strong> assess and fix the specific data your priority use cases need (<a href="/ai-consultancy/data-strategy/">data strategy</a>) — you rarely need to fix everything first.</li><li><strong>Skills:</strong> provide practical, role-based <a href="/ai-consultancy/ai-training/">training</a> and ongoing support so adoption sticks.</li><li><strong>Trust:</strong> build proportionate <a href="/ai-consultancy/ai-governance/">governance</a> — guardrails, human oversight and compliance — so people can act confidently.</li><li><strong>ROI:</strong> prove value with a low-risk <a href="/ai-consultancy/ai-proof-of-concept/">pilot</a> and estimate returns with the <a href="/tools/ai-roi-calculator/">AI ROI Calculator</a>.</li><li><strong>Change:</strong> involve people early, show them the benefit to their own work, and lead the change rather than mandating it.</li></ul>
<h2>Adopt AI, for real</h2>
<p>Alugence helps you clear the barriers and get AI working. Explore <a href="/ai-consultancy/">AI consultancy</a> or <a href="/contact/">book a call</a>.</p>',
		),
		array(
			'slug' => 'ai-agents-examples', 'cpt' => 'post',
			'title' => 'AI agents explained (with examples)',
			'meta_title' => 'AI Agents Explained With Examples | Alugence',
			'meta_desc' => 'AI agents explained with real business examples — from customer service to finance and sales. See what AI agents do and where they add value.',
			'excerpt' => 'An AI agent is a system that pursues a goal by planning steps and taking actions across tools, with limited human input. In business, AI agents handle multi-step tasks like triaging support tickets, processing invoices, qualifying leads, and researching topics — completing the work, not just answering questions.',
			'faqs' => array(array('What is an example of an AI agent?','A customer-service AI agent is a common example: it reads an incoming enquiry, checks the customer\'s account, drafts a personalised reply, and books a follow-up — escalating anything sensitive to a human. It completes the task rather than just answering a question.'),array('What can AI agents do in business?','AI agents can triage and resolve support tickets, process invoices, qualify and route sales leads, research and summarise information, and monitor operations for exceptions. They handle multi-step tasks across systems, freeing staff from repetitive work.'),array('What\'s the difference between an AI agent and a chatbot?','A chatbot responds to messages, mostly with information; an AI agent pursues goals by planning, using tools and taking actions across systems to complete tasks. Every agent can converse, but not every chatbot can act.'),array('Are AI agents reliable enough for real work?','Yes, when built with guardrails, human approval for high-stakes steps, and monitoring. Well-designed agents handle the routine load and escalate uncertainty to a person, so reliability and accountability are maintained.')),
			'html' => '<nav class="on-this-page" aria-label="On this page"><a href="#what">What an AI agent is</a> · <a href="#examples">Examples by function</a> · <a href="#when">When to use an agent</a> · <a href="#faqs">FAQs</a></nav>
<h2 id="what">What is an AI agent?</h2>
<p>An AI agent is built around a large language model that can reason about a task, use tools (search, databases, apps), and act on the results — repeating until the goal is met. Unlike a chatbot that only replies, an agent <em>does things</em>. For the full concept, see <a href="/guides/what-is-agentic-ai/">What is agentic AI?</a></p>
<h2 id="examples">AI agent examples by function</h2>
<ul><li><strong>Customer service:</strong> an agent reads an enquiry, checks the customer\'s account, drafts a personalised reply, and books a follow-up — escalating anything sensitive.</li><li><strong>Finance &amp; admin:</strong> an agent processes invoices, extracting data, matching to purchase orders, and flagging discrepancies for a human.</li><li><strong>Sales:</strong> an agent qualifies inbound leads, enriches them with data, and schedules meetings with the right rep.</li><li><strong>Research:</strong> an agent gathers and summarises sources on a topic and produces a briefing.</li><li><strong>IT support:</strong> an agent triages tickets, resolves common issues automatically, and routes the rest.</li><li><strong>Operations:</strong> an agent monitors a process, spots exceptions, and takes or recommends corrective action.</li></ul>
<h2 id="when">When to use an AI agent</h2>
<p>AI agents shine on tasks that are multi-step, high-volume, and involve pulling information from several places — exactly the work that clogs up teams. They\'re less suited to one-off or highly sensitive decisions, where a simpler tool or human judgement is better. Alugence builds agents through <a href="/ai-consultancy/ai-automation/">AI automation &amp; agents</a>, usually starting with a <a href="/ai-consultancy/ai-proof-of-concept/">pilot</a>.</p>
<h2>Put an AI agent to work</h2>
<p>Alugence designs AI agents around your real tasks. Explore <a href="/ai-consultancy/ai-automation/">AI automation &amp; agents</a> or <a href="/contact/">book a call</a>.</p>',
		),
		array(
			'slug' => 'ai-automation-use-cases', 'cpt' => 'post',
			'title' => 'AI automation use cases by function',
			'meta_title' => 'AI Automation Use Cases by Function | Alugence',
			'meta_desc' => 'AI automation use cases by business function — finance, HR, sales, marketing, operations and support. Find where AI can save you the most time.',
			'excerpt' => 'AI automation can take repetitive work off almost every team. Common use cases by function include invoice processing in finance, CV screening in HR, lead qualification in sales, content and reporting in marketing, ticket triage in support, and document processing in operations. The best place to start is wherever your team loses the most time to routine tasks.',
			'faqs' => array(array('What are common AI automation use cases?','Common use cases include invoice processing in finance, CV screening in HR, lead qualification in sales, content and reporting in marketing, ticket triage in support, and document processing in operations. Broadly, any high-volume, repetitive, admin-heavy task is a candidate.'),array('Which department benefits most from AI automation?','It varies by business, but finance, customer support and operations often see the fastest returns because they carry high volumes of repetitive, rules-guided work. The best answer comes from mapping where your specific teams lose the most time.'),array('How do I choose the first process to automate?','Pick a task that\'s high-volume, repetitive and well-defined, scoring candidates on value, effort and risk. Starting with the best-balanced quick win proves value fast and builds momentum for wider automation.'),array('Can AI automation work across multiple departments?','Yes. Once you\'ve proven a pattern in one team, the same approach often transfers to others, and AI agents can work across systems that span departments. Many organisations expand automation function by function after an initial win.')),
			'html' => '<nav class="on-this-page" aria-label="On this page"><a href="#functions">By function</a> · <a href="#prioritise">How to prioritise</a> · <a href="#faqs">FAQs</a></nav>
<h2 id="functions">AI automation use cases by department</h2>
<ul><li><strong>Finance:</strong> invoice processing, expense checking, reconciliation, financial reporting.</li><li><strong>HR &amp; recruitment:</strong> CV screening, candidate comms, onboarding admin, policy Q&amp;A.</li><li><strong>Sales:</strong> lead qualification and enrichment, meeting scheduling, proposal drafting, CRM updates.</li><li><strong>Marketing:</strong> content drafting, campaign reporting, <a href="/ai-search/ai-search-visibility/">AI search visibility</a>, analytics.</li><li><strong>Customer support:</strong> ticket triage, <a href="/ai-consultancy/ai-chatbots/">chatbot</a> responses, knowledge-base answers, escalation routing.</li><li><strong>Operations:</strong> document processing, data entry, process monitoring, exception handling.</li><li><strong>Legal &amp; compliance:</strong> contract review, document summarisation, compliance checks.</li></ul>
<h2 id="prioritise">How to prioritise use cases</h2>
<p>Start where tasks are high-volume, repetitive and rules-guided or admin-heavy — that\'s where AI automation pays back fastest. Score candidates on value (time or money saved), effort (how hard to build), and risk (what happens if it\'s wrong), then begin with the best-balanced quick win. The <a href="/tools/ai-roi-calculator/">AI ROI Calculator</a> helps estimate the value.</p>
<h2>Find your best automation opportunities</h2>
<p>Alugence maps and builds the automations with the fastest payback. Explore <a href="/ai-consultancy/ai-automation/">AI automation</a> or <a href="/contact/">book a call</a>.</p>',
		),
		array(
			'slug' => 'ai-consulting-cost', 'cpt' => 'post',
			'title' => 'How much does AI consulting cost?',
			'meta_title' => 'How Much Does AI Consulting Cost? | Alugence',
			'meta_desc' => 'How much does AI consulting cost? Typical pricing models and ranges for readiness assessments, pilots, builds and retainers explained.',
			'excerpt' => 'AI consulting is usually priced by engagement type. Fixed-fee readiness assessments typically start from around £3,500 / $4,500, pilots from £15,000 / $19,000, and production builds from £40,000 / $50,000. Ongoing support runs as a monthly retainer (from ~£3,000 / $3,800) or a day rate (~£950–£1,500 / $1,200–$1,900). Actual cost depends on scope, complexity and data readiness.',
			'faqs' => array(array('How much does AI consulting cost?','AI consulting is typically priced by engagement: fixed-fee readiness assessments from around £3,500 / $4,500, pilots from £15,000 / $19,000, and production builds from £40,000 / $50,000, plus retainers and day rates. The exact cost depends on scope, complexity and data readiness.'),array('Why does AI consulting cost vary so much?','Because scope varies enormously — a two-week assessment is very different from a multi-month enterprise build with deep integration and governance. Data readiness, integration depth and regulatory requirements all move the price. That\'s why most consultancies quote "from" figures and confirm after scoping.'),array('Is there a cheap way to start with AI consulting?','Yes — a fixed-fee readiness assessment or a small pilot lets you start low-risk and prove value before committing to a larger build. The free AI ROI Calculator is an even cheaper first step to build the business case.'),array('Do AI consultants charge fixed fees or day rates?','Both. Most scoped work is fixed-fee for budget certainty, while advisory or exploratory work may use a day rate. Ongoing support and leadership are usually monthly retainers. See Alugence\'s pricing for examples.')),
			'html' => '<nav class="on-this-page" aria-label="On this page"><a href="#models">Pricing models</a> · <a href="#ranges">Typical ranges</a> · <a href="#drivers">What drives cost</a> · <a href="#worth">Is it worth it?</a> · <a href="#faqs">FAQs</a></nav>
<h2 id="models">How AI consulting is priced</h2>
<ul><li><strong>Fixed-fee packages</strong> — defined scope and deliverables (assessments, pilots, builds).</li><li><strong>Monthly retainers</strong> — ongoing support or leadership (e.g. a <a href="/ai-consultancy/fractional-ai-officer/">Fractional CAIO</a>).</li><li><strong>Day rates</strong> — advisory or workshops where a fixed fee isn\'t practical.</li></ul>
<h2 id="ranges">Typical AI consulting cost ranges</h2>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Engagement</th><th>Indicative from (GBP)</th><th>Indicative from (USD)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>AI readiness assessment / sprint</td><td>£3,500</td><td>$4,500</td></tr><tr><td>Discovery &amp; pilot (PoC)</td><td>£15,000</td><td>$19,000</td></tr><tr><td>Production build &amp; embed</td><td>£40,000</td><td>$50,000</td></tr><tr><td>Fractional CAIO (monthly)</td><td>£3,000/mo</td><td>$3,800/mo</td></tr><tr><td>Day rate</td><td>£950–£1,500</td><td>$1,200–$1,900</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p><em>Indicative benchmarks — confirm against Alugence\'s <a href="/pricing/">pricing</a>.</em></p>
<h2 id="drivers">What drives the cost</h2>
<ul><li><strong>Scope &amp; complexity</strong> — more use cases and integrations cost more.</li><li><strong>Data readiness</strong> — messy data adds preparation work.</li><li><strong>Integration depth</strong> — connecting to more systems increases effort.</li><li><strong>Governance needs</strong> — regulated environments require more controls.</li><li><strong>Ongoing vs one-off</strong> — retainers spread cost; projects are fixed.</li></ul>
<h2 id="worth">Is AI consulting worth the cost?</h2>
<p>It\'s worth it when the value created exceeds the cost — which is why efficiency projects, with payback often inside 6–12 months, are a common starting point. Estimate your potential saving with the <a href="/tools/ai-roi-calculator/">AI ROI Calculator</a>, and read <a href="/blog/ai-roi/">AI ROI: how to measure it</a>. Starting with a low-cost readiness assessment or pilot de-risks the bigger spend.</p>
<h2>See transparent pricing</h2>
<p>Alugence publishes indicative pricing for both practices. See <a href="/pricing/">pricing</a> or <a href="/contact/">book a scoping call</a>.</p>',
		),
		array(
			'slug' => 'ai-readiness-checklist', 'cpt' => 'post',
			'title' => 'AI readiness assessment: a 10-point checklist',
			'meta_title' => 'AI Readiness Checklist: 10 Points | Alugence',
			'meta_desc' => 'Is your business ready for AI? Use this 10-point AI readiness checklist to assess your data, tools, team and strategy before you invest.',
			'excerpt' => 'A business is ready for AI when it has a clear goal, usable data, suitable tools, capable and willing people, and governance in place. This 10-point checklist covers data, tooling, team, strategy and governance so you can judge your readiness — and see what to fix first — before investing.',
			'faqs' => array(array('How do I know if my business is ready for AI?','You\'re ready when you have a clear goal, a prioritised use case, usable and governed data, suitable tools, capable and willing people, leadership backing, budget, a risk view, and a way to measure success. Working through a checklist like the 10 points above shows where you stand.'),array('What is the most common AI readiness gap?','Data is the most common gap — data that\'s siloed, messy or hard to access blocks otherwise-viable use cases. Adoption readiness (skills and willingness) is the second. Both are fixable, and worth addressing before large AI spend.'),array('Do I need to fix everything before starting AI?','No. You usually sequence foundational work — often data — alongside a low-risk quick win, rather than waiting for perfection. A readiness assessment identifies what must be fixed first versus what can improve as you go.'),array('How is this checklist different from a full assessment?','The checklist is a quick self-evaluation; a full AI readiness assessment is a consultant-led deep dive into your actual data, tools and processes that produces a scored, costed plan and prioritised use cases you can act on.')),
			'html' => '<nav class="on-this-page" aria-label="On this page"><a href="#checklist">The checklist</a> · <a href="#score">Scoring your readiness</a> · <a href="#faqs">FAQs</a></nav>
<h2 id="checklist">The 10-point AI readiness checklist</h2>
<ol><li><strong>Clear business goal.</strong> You know the outcome AI should improve (cost, revenue, service).</li><li><strong>A prioritised use case.</strong> You\'ve identified at least one high-value, well-defined use case.</li><li><strong>Usable data.</strong> The data the use case needs exists, is accessible and reasonably clean.</li><li><strong>Data governance.</strong> You know who owns data and can use it compliantly (GDPR).</li><li><strong>Suitable tools &amp; systems.</strong> Your systems can integrate with AI, or you know the gap.</li><li><strong>Team capability.</strong> Someone can lead adoption, and staff are open to it.</li><li><strong>Leadership backing.</strong> Decision-makers support the investment and change.</li><li><strong>Realistic budget.</strong> You can fund a pilot and, if it works, a build.</li><li><strong>Governance &amp; risk view.</strong> You\'ve considered risk, oversight and compliance.</li><li><strong>A way to measure success.</strong> You\'ve defined the metrics that prove ROI.</li></ol>
<h2 id="score">Scoring your readiness</h2>
<p>Count how many points you can confidently tick. <strong>8–10:</strong> you\'re ready to pilot or scale. <strong>5–7:</strong> address the gaps (often data or tooling) alongside a small quick win. <strong>0–4:</strong> shore up foundations — usually data, goals and leadership — before investing heavily. For an instant, structured score, use the <a href="/tools/ai-roi-calculator/">AI ROI Calculator</a>; for a consultant-led deep dive, see the <a href="/ai-consultancy/ai-readiness/">AI readiness assessment</a>.</p>
<h2>Get a proper readiness assessment</h2>
<p>Alugence\'s <a href="/ai-consultancy/ai-readiness/">AI readiness assessment</a> turns this checklist into a costed plan. <a href="/contact/">Book yours</a>.</p>',
		),
		array(
			'slug' => 'ai-roadmap', 'cpt' => 'post',
			'title' => 'Building an AI roadmap',
			'meta_title' => 'Building an AI Roadmap | Alugence',
			'meta_desc' => 'How to build an AI roadmap: a step-by-step approach to sequencing AI use cases, foundations and quick wins into a costed, deliverable plan.',
			'excerpt' => 'An AI roadmap is a sequenced, costed plan for adopting AI — which use cases to pursue, in what order, with what data, governance and resources, and how success is measured. You build one by anchoring to business goals, prioritising use cases on value, effort and risk, and sequencing quick wins alongside foundational work.',
			'faqs' => array(array('What is an AI roadmap?','An AI roadmap is a sequenced, costed plan for adopting AI — the use cases to pursue, their order, the data and governance they need, owners, and success metrics. It turns an AI strategy into something a business can actually deliver and track.'),array('How do you build an AI roadmap?','Anchor it to business goals, discover and prioritise use cases by value, effort and risk, map the data and governance dependencies, then sequence delivery — quick wins first, foundations in parallel, bigger bets staged — with owners and metrics for each phase.'),array('How far ahead should an AI roadmap plan?','Most AI roadmaps plan 12–18 months in phases, with near-term work detailed and later phases lighter, because AI moves fast. Review the roadmap regularly and adapt as you learn and as the technology evolves.'),array('Who should own the AI roadmap?','Senior leadership should own it, often supported by an AI lead such as a Fractional Chief AI Officer. Ownership matters because a roadmap only delivers value if someone drives it and keeps it current.')),
			'html' => '<nav class="on-this-page" aria-label="On this page"><a href="#what">What it is</a> · <a href="#build">How to build one</a> · <a href="#example">Example structure</a> · <a href="#faqs">FAQs</a></nav>
<h2 id="what">What is an AI roadmap?</h2>
<p>An AI roadmap turns an AI strategy into an executable plan. It lays out the use cases you\'ll pursue, their sequence, the data and governance foundations they depend on, who owns them, and how you\'ll measure success. It\'s the difference between "we should use AI" and a plan leadership can fund and track. See <a href="/guides/ai-strategy-framework/">What is an AI strategy?</a></p>
<h2 id="build">How to build an AI roadmap</h2>
<ol><li><strong>Anchor to goals.</strong> Start from the business outcomes you care about.</li><li><strong>Discover use cases</strong> across functions.</li><li><strong>Prioritise</strong> each on value, effort and risk.</li><li><strong>Map dependencies</strong> — the data, tooling and governance each needs.</li><li><strong>Sequence</strong> — quick wins first, foundations in parallel, bigger bets staged.</li><li><strong>Assign owners and metrics</strong> for each phase.</li><li><strong>Review and adapt</strong> as you learn.</li></ol>
<h2 id="example">An example roadmap structure</h2>
<ul><li><strong>Phase 1 (0–3 months):</strong> readiness assessment, one or two quick-win automations, data gap fixes.</li><li><strong>Phase 2 (3–9 months):</strong> pilots on higher-value use cases, governance framework, early integrations.</li><li><strong>Phase 3 (9–18 months):</strong> production builds, scaling proven use cases, ongoing optimisation.</li></ul>
<h2>Get a roadmap you can deliver</h2>
<p>Alugence builds costed AI roadmaps and helps deliver them. Explore <a href="/ai-consultancy/ai-strategy/">AI strategy</a> or <a href="/contact/">book a call</a>.</p>',
		),
		array(
			'slug' => 'ai-roi', 'cpt' => 'post',
			'title' => 'AI ROI: how to measure it',
			'meta_title' => 'AI ROI: How to Measure It | Alugence',
			'meta_desc' => 'How to measure AI ROI — the value drivers, a simple formula, and how to build a credible business case for your AI investment.',
			'excerpt' => 'AI ROI is measured by comparing the value AI creates — hours saved, revenue gained, costs avoided — against the cost of building and running it. A simple formula is (value created − total cost) ÷ total cost. Efficiency projects often target payback within 6–12 months. The key is defining measurable value before you start.',
			'faqs' => array(array('How do you measure AI ROI?','Measure AI ROI by comparing the value created — hours saved, revenue gained, costs avoided — against the total cost to build and run the solution, using (value − cost) ÷ cost. Define the value metric and a baseline before you start so you can prove the difference.'),array('What is a typical payback period for AI?','Efficiency and automation projects often target payback within 6–12 months, though it varies with scope and complexity. Comparing your estimated annual value against implementation cost gives a rough payback period; a pilot refines it with real data.'),array('How do I build a business case for AI?','Pick a metric leadership cares about, measure a baseline, run a low-risk pilot, and compare the result against the baseline. Be honest about all costs and conservative on savings. A credible, measured case earns support for further investment.'),array('Is AI ROI hard to measure?','Time-saving and cost-avoidance benefits are straightforward to quantify; softer benefits like risk reduction and improved experience are harder but still real. Starting with an easily measured efficiency use case makes your first ROI case clear and defensible.')),
			'html' => '<nav class="on-this-page" aria-label="On this page"><a href="#drivers">Value drivers</a> · <a href="#calc">How to calculate</a> · <a href="#case">Building the business case</a> · <a href="#faqs">FAQs</a></nav>
<h2 id="drivers">Where AI creates measurable value</h2>
<ul><li><strong>Time saved</strong> — hours of manual work removed (the easiest to quantify).</li><li><strong>Cost avoided</strong> — reduced errors, rework, or headcount growth.</li><li><strong>Revenue gained</strong> — more leads captured, higher conversion, faster service.</li><li><strong>Risk reduced</strong> — fewer compliance or quality failures (harder to quantify but real).</li></ul>
<h2 id="calc">How to calculate AI ROI</h2>
<p>A simple approach: estimate the annual value created, subtract the total cost (build plus run), and divide by the total cost.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> if an automation saves 28 hours a week at £25/hour, that\'s ~£36,400 a year. If it cost £30,000 to build and £5,000/year to run, first-year ROI is roughly (£36,400 − £35,000) ÷ £35,000 ≈ 4%, rising sharply in year two once the build cost is behind you. <code>[EXAMPLE figures — use the AI ROI Calculator for your own inputs.]</code></p>
<p>Estimate your own with the <a href="/tools/ai-roi-calculator/">AI ROI Calculator</a>.</p>
<h2 id="case">Building a credible AI business case</h2>
<p>Define the metric before you build, measure a baseline, run a pilot, and compare results against the baseline. A credible case is honest about costs (including change and maintenance), conservative on savings, and tied to a metric leadership already cares about. Under-promise and measure — it builds trust for the next investment.</p>
<h2>Estimate your AI ROI</h2>
<p>Use Alugence\'s free <a href="/tools/ai-roi-calculator/">AI ROI Calculator</a>, then <a href="/contact/">book a call</a> to validate the numbers.</p>',
		),
		array(
			'slug' => 'ai-seo-vs-traditional-seo', 'cpt' => 'post',
			'title' => 'AI SEO vs traditional SEO',
			'meta_title' => 'AI SEO vs Traditional SEO | Alugence',
			'meta_desc' => 'AI SEO vs traditional SEO compared: what\'s changed, what\'s the same, and how to optimise for both Google rankings and AI citations.',
			'excerpt' => 'Traditional SEO optimises web pages to rank as links in search results. AI SEO extends that to the AI era — optimising to be cited in AI answers (ChatGPT, Perplexity, Google AI Overviews) and using AI tools to do SEO faster. The fundamentals of quality, structure and authority still apply; AI SEO adds entity clarity, answer-first content and citation building.',
			'faqs' => array(array('Is traditional SEO dead?','No. Rankings, quality content and technical health still matter and often feed the signals AI engines use. What\'s changed is that being cited in AI answers now matters alongside ranking links. AI SEO evolves traditional SEO rather than replacing it.'),array('What is the difference between AI SEO and SEO?','Traditional SEO optimises pages to rank as links; AI SEO also optimises to be cited in AI-generated answers and uses AI tools to work faster. AI SEO adds entity clarity, answer-first content, schema and citation building on top of SEO fundamentals.'),array('Should I still invest in traditional SEO?','Yes — it\'s the foundation AI SEO builds on. Strong content, authority and technical health support both classic rankings and AI citations. The smart move is to keep investing in SEO while adding AI search optimisation.'),array('How do I optimise for both Google and AI answers?','Maintain SEO fundamentals, then make content answer-first and extractable, keep your brand entity consistent, add FAQ and schema, allow AI crawlers, and track your AI citations. One modern strategy serves both. See AI SEO.')),
			'html' => '<nav class="on-this-page" aria-label="On this page"><a href="#same">What\'s the same</a> · <a href="#different">What\'s different</a> · <a href="#table">Comparison</a> · <a href="#do">What to do</a> · <a href="#faqs">FAQs</a></nav>
<h2 id="same">What stays the same</h2>
<p>Both disciplines reward the same fundamentals: genuinely useful content, clear structure, technical health (speed, crawlability, mobile), and authority/E-E-A-T. AI engines often draw on the same quality signals as Google\'s ranking systems, so strong traditional SEO underpins AI SEO rather than being replaced by it.</p>
<h2 id="different">What\'s different in AI SEO</h2>
<p>AI SEO adds new goals and tactics: being <em>cited inside an answer</em> (not just ranking a link), making your brand <strong>entity</strong> machine-legible, writing <strong>answer-first</strong> content that AI can extract, ensuring AI crawlers can read your site, and building citations across trusted sources. It also means using AI tools to accelerate research and content — with human quality control.</p>
<h2 id="table">AI SEO vs traditional SEO at a glance</h2>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Traditional SEO</th><th>AI SEO</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Primary goal</strong></td><td>Rank links in search</td><td>Rank links <em>and</em> get cited in AI answers</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Targets</strong></td><td>Google, Bing results</td><td>+ ChatGPT, Perplexity, Gemini, AI Overviews</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Extra levers</strong></td><td>—</td><td>Entity clarity, answer-first content, citations, llms.txt</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Tooling</strong></td><td>SEO tools</td><td>+ AI-assisted research &amp; content, AI visibility trackers</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Measured by</strong></td><td>Rankings, clicks</td><td>+ mentions, citations, share of voice</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<h2 id="do">What to do about it</h2>
<p>Don\'t abandon SEO — evolve it. Keep your technical and content foundations strong, then layer AI search optimisation: fix your brand entity, make content answer-first, add schema and FAQs, allow AI crawlers, and track your AI citations. This is exactly what <a href="/ai-search/ai-seo/">AI SEO</a> as a discipline covers, bridging into <a href="/ai-search/generative-engine-optimisation/">GEO</a> and <a href="/ai-search/answer-engine-optimisation/">AEO</a>.</p>
<h2>Modernise your SEO for AI</h2>
<p>Alugence\'s <a href="/ai-search/ai-seo/">AI SEO consultancy</a> bridges classic SEO and AI search. <a href="/contact/">Book a call</a>.</p>',
		),
