		array(
			'slug' => 'business-process-automation-ai', 'cpt' => 'post',
			'title' => 'Business process automation with AI',
			'meta_title' => 'Business Process Automation With AI | Alugence',
			'meta_desc' => 'How AI is changing business process automation — handling unstructured work traditional BPA can\'t. Learn what to automate and how to start.',
			'excerpt' => 'Business process automation (BPA) with AI uses artificial intelligence to automate end-to-end processes — including the unstructured, judgement-based steps traditional automation can\'t handle. Where classic BPA follows fixed rules, AI-powered BPA can read documents, make decisions and adapt, automating far more of a process.',
			'faqs' => array(array('What is business process automation with AI?','It\'s using AI to automate end-to-end business processes, including the unstructured, judgement-based steps traditional automation can\'t handle. AI can read documents, classify information, make decisions and manage exceptions, so much more of a process runs without manual effort.'),array('How is AI-powered BPA different from RPA?','RPA automates structured, rules-based steps by mimicking human actions in software; AI-powered BPA adds the ability to handle unstructured inputs and make judgements. Combining them automates more of a process — RPA for predictable steps, AI for the rest.'),array('What processes are best for AI-powered automation?','Document-heavy, high-volume processes with unstructured inputs — invoice handling, onboarding, claims, support triage — are strong candidates, because AI can absorb the reading and decision-making that stalled traditional automation. Map your process to find the bottlenecks.'),array('How do I start automating a business process with AI?','Map the process, identify the manual and unstructured bottlenecks, design automation with guardrails and human checkpoints, pilot it on real cases, then scale. Starting with a single high-value process proves the value before you expand.')),
			'html' => '<nav class="on-this-page" aria-label="On this page"><a href="#what">What it is</a> · <a href="#vs">AI vs traditional BPA</a> · <a href="#start">How to start</a> · <a href="#faqs">FAQs</a></nav>
<h2 id="what">What is business process automation with AI?</h2>
<p>Business process automation is the use of technology to run processes with minimal human effort. Traditionally, BPA handled the structured, rules-based parts of a process and left the rest to people. Adding AI extends automation to the messy parts — reading emails and documents, classifying, deciding, and handling exceptions — so more of the end-to-end process runs automatically.</p>
<h2 id="vs">AI-powered BPA vs traditional BPA</h2>
<p>Traditional BPA is reliable for predictable steps but stalls on anything unstructured. AI-powered BPA understands unstructured inputs and makes judgements, so it can automate processes that used to need constant human handling. Often the two combine — rules for the predictable steps, AI for the rest. See <a href="/blog/agentic-ai-vs-automation/">Agentic AI vs traditional automation</a>.</p>
<h2 id="start">How to start with AI-powered BPA</h2>
<ol><li><strong>Map the process</strong> end to end and find the manual bottlenecks.</li><li><strong>Identify the unstructured steps</strong> AI could handle (reading, deciding).</li><li><strong>Design</strong> the automation with guardrails and human checkpoints.</li><li><strong>Pilot</strong> on real cases and measure time saved.</li><li><strong>Scale</strong> across the process and to similar processes.</li></ol>
<p>Alugence delivers this through <a href="/ai-consultancy/ai-automation/">AI automation</a>, usually starting with a <a href="/ai-consultancy/ai-proof-of-concept/">pilot</a>.</p>
<h2>Automate more of your processes</h2>
<p>Alugence automates the parts of your processes that used to need people. Explore <a href="/ai-consultancy/ai-automation/">AI automation</a> or <a href="/contact/">book a call</a>.</p>',
		),
		array(
			'slug' => 'how-to-choose-ai-consultancy', 'cpt' => 'post',
			'title' => 'How to choose an AI consultancy',
			'meta_title' => 'How to Choose an AI Consultancy | Alugence',
			'meta_desc' => 'How to choose an AI consultancy: the questions to ask, red flags to avoid, and what a good AI consultant should deliver. A practical guide.',
			'excerpt' => 'Choose an AI consultancy on evidence of delivery, not hype. Look for a clear, low-risk starting point (like a readiness assessment or pilot), named senior consultants, real outcomes, strong governance, and honesty about what AI can\'t do. Avoid firms that promise guaranteed results, hide who does the work, or lead with technology instead of your goals.',
			'faqs' => array(array('How do I choose the right AI consultancy?','Choose based on evidence of delivery: a low-risk starting point, named senior consultants, real measurable outcomes, strong governance, and honesty about limitations. Ask who will do the work, how they measure success, and what happens if a pilot fails.'),array('What questions should I ask an AI consultant?','Ask who will work on your project and their experience, the lowest-risk way to start, evidence of similar outcomes, how they handle data and compliance, how they measure ROI, what happens if a pilot doesn\'t work, and whether they support adoption and training.'),array('What are the warning signs of a bad AI consultancy?','Warning signs include guaranteed results, hype without evidence, pushing straight to a large build with no low-risk start, vagueness about who does the work, ignoring governance and data, and an unwillingness to acknowledge what AI can\'t do.'),array('Should I choose a specialist or a generalist consultancy?','It depends on your need. A specialist offers depth in a specific area; a broad consultancy can join up strategy, build and adoption. What matters most is evidence of relevant delivery and a sensible, low-risk way to start. See how to choose.')),
			'html' => '<nav class="on-this-page" aria-label="On this page"><a href="#good">What good looks like</a> · <a href="#questions">Questions to ask</a> · <a href="#redflags">Red flags</a> · <a href="#faqs">FAQs</a></nav>
<h2 id="good">What a good AI consultancy looks like</h2>
<ul><li><strong>Starts with your goals, not tools.</strong> They ask what you\'re trying to achieve first.</li><li><strong>Offers a low-risk start.</strong> A readiness assessment or pilot before a big commitment.</li><li><strong>Names the people doing the work.</strong> Senior consultants, not an anonymous team.</li><li><strong>Shows real outcomes.</strong> Case studies with actual, measurable results.</li><li><strong>Takes governance seriously.</strong> Data protection, risk and compliance built in.</li><li><strong>Is honest.</strong> Clear about limitations, risks and what AI won\'t fix.</li></ul>
<h2 id="questions">Questions to ask an AI consultancy</h2>
<ol><li>Who exactly will work on our project, and what\'s their experience?</li><li>What\'s the smallest, lowest-risk way to start with you?</li><li>Can you show outcomes from similar clients or sectors?</li><li>How do you handle data protection, security and compliance?</li><li>How do you measure success and ROI?</li><li>What happens if a pilot doesn\'t work?</li><li>Do you help with adoption and training, not just the build?</li></ol>
<h2 id="redflags">Red flags to avoid</h2>
<ul><li>Guarantees of specific results or "AI will transform everything" hype.</li><li>No clear starting point — pushing straight to a large, expensive build.</li><li>Vagueness about who does the work or how success is measured.</li><li>Ignoring governance, data and change management.</li><li>No willingness to say what AI can\'t do for you.</li></ul>
<h2>Talk to a consultancy that starts small</h2>
<p>Alugence begins with a low-risk assessment and names the consultants who\'ll do your work. <a href="/contact/">Book a discovery call</a>.</p>',
		),
		array(
			'slug' => 'in-house-vs-ai-consultancy', 'cpt' => 'post',
			'title' => 'In-house AI team vs AI consultancy',
			'meta_title' => 'In-House AI Team vs AI Consultancy | Alugence',
			'meta_desc' => 'In-house AI team vs AI consultancy: the costs, speed, risks and trade-offs of each — and how to decide (or combine both) for your business.',
			'excerpt' => 'Building an in-house AI team gives you deep, permanent capability but is slow and expensive to hire and retain. An AI consultancy gives you senior expertise and faster delivery without the fixed cost, but less permanent ownership. Many businesses combine both — a consultancy to start and upskill, an in-house team to sustain.',
			'faqs' => array(array('Should I build an in-house AI team or hire a consultancy?','It depends on your needs. Build in-house when AI is core and you have continuous work and can retain talent; use a consultancy to start fast, access broad expertise, and de-risk projects without fixed cost. Many businesses combine both — consultancy first, in-house team over time.'),array('Is an AI consultancy cheaper than hiring?','Often, in the near term — you avoid recruitment cost, salaries, and the risk of a mis-hire, and pay for outcomes or a retainer instead. Over the long run, an in-house team can be more cost-effective for continuous core work. The right answer depends on how much ongoing AI work you have.'),array('Can a consultancy help us build our own AI team?','Yes. A good consultancy delivers *and* upskills your people, transferring knowledge and setting up ways of working so you can sustain AI internally. This hybrid approach gives you speed now and lasting capability. See Alugence\'s AI training.'),array('How do I decide between the two?','Weigh how core AI is to your business, how much continuous work you have, your ability to hire and retain talent, and how fast you need results. If you need momentum now or lack scarce skills, start with a consultancy; combine with in-house hiring as your needs mature.')),
			'html' => '<nav class="on-this-page" aria-label="On this page"><a href="#table">Comparison</a> · <a href="#inhouse">When to build in-house</a> · <a href="#consultancy">When to use a consultancy</a> · <a href="#hybrid">The hybrid model</a> · <a href="#faqs">FAQs</a></nav>
<h2 id="table">In-house vs consultancy at a glance</h2>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>In-house AI team</th><th>AI consultancy</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Speed to start</strong></td><td>Slow (hiring)</td><td>Fast</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Cost model</strong></td><td>Fixed salaries</td><td>Project or retainer</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Expertise breadth</strong></td><td>Limited to hires</td><td>Broad, cross-sector</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Permanent capability</strong></td><td>High</td><td>Lower (but can upskill you)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Risk</strong></td><td>Hiring risk, hard to retain</td><td>Dependency on partner</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Best for</strong></td><td>Ongoing, core AI needs</td><td>Getting started, specific projects, leadership</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<h2 id="inhouse">When to build an in-house team</h2>
<p>Build in-house when AI is core to your product or operations, you have continuous work to justify permanent roles, and you can attract and retain scarce talent. It gives you deep institutional knowledge — but hiring takes months, senior AI talent is expensive and competitive, and a single hire can\'t cover strategy, engineering, data and governance alone.</p>
<h2 id="consultancy">When to use an AI consultancy</h2>
<p>Use a consultancy to start quickly, access a breadth of expertise, and de-risk your first projects without a large fixed cost. It\'s ideal for getting momentum, delivering specific projects, and providing senior leadership — for example a <a href="/ai-consultancy/fractional-ai-officer/">Fractional Chief AI Officer</a> — while you decide what to build permanently.</p>
<h2 id="hybrid">The hybrid model (often best)</h2>
<p>Many businesses combine both: a consultancy provides strategy, delivery and leadership early and upskills your people, while you gradually build an in-house team to sustain and extend the work. This gives you speed now and capability later. Alugence works this way — delivering <em>and</em> enabling your team through <a href="/ai-consultancy/ai-training/">training</a>.</p>
<h2>Get moving without the hiring wait</h2>
<p>Alugence delivers AI now and upskills your team for later. Explore <a href="/ai-consultancy/">AI consultancy</a> or <a href="/contact/">book a call</a>.</p>',
		),
		array(
			'slug' => 'llms-txt-guide', 'cpt' => 'post',
			'title' => 'llms.txt: what it is and how to use it',
			'meta_title' => 'llms.txt: What It Is & How to Use It',
			'meta_desc' => 'llms.txt is a plain-text file that helps AI crawlers understand your site. Learn what llms.txt is, what to include, and how to use it.',
			'excerpt' => 'llms.txt is a plain-text file placed at the root of your website (yoursite.com/llms.txt) that gives AI crawlers a concise summary of your brand and a curated list of your most important URLs. It\'s a proposed standard to help large language models find, understand and cite your content more accurately.',
			'faqs' => array(array('What is llms.txt?','llms.txt is a plain-text, Markdown-formatted file at your website\'s root that gives AI crawlers a summary of your brand and a curated list of key URLs. It helps large language models find, understand and cite your content more accurately.'),array('Where do I put the llms.txt file?','At the root of your domain, so it\'s reachable at yoursite.com/llms.txt — the same location convention as robots.txt. Make sure it returns as plain text and is publicly accessible.'),array('Is llms.txt an official standard?','It\'s a proposed, community-driven convention that\'s gaining adoption, not a formal requirement enforced by AI vendors. Support varies, but publishing one is low-effort and can only help models understand your site — a sensible part of LLM optimisation.'),array('Does llms.txt guarantee AI will cite me?','No. llms.txt helps models find and understand your content, but citations still depend on your content being accessible, extractable and authoritative. Treat it as one helpful signal alongside entity clarity, structure and off-site citations. See how to get cited by AI.')),
			'html' => '<nav class="on-this-page" aria-label="On this page"><a href="#what">What it is</a> · <a href="#why">Why it matters</a> · <a href="#include">What to include</a> · <a href="#create">How to create one</a> · <a href="#faqs">FAQs</a></nav>
<h2 id="what">What is llms.txt?</h2>
<p>llms.txt is a simple Markdown-formatted text file, served at your domain\'s root, that tells AI systems what your site is about and points them to the pages that matter most. Think of it as a friendly map for LLMs — analogous to robots.txt or a sitemap, but aimed at helping AI models understand and represent your brand rather than governing crawling.</p>
<h2 id="why">Why llms.txt matters for AI search</h2>
<p>AI crawlers have limited time and don\'t always parse complex, JavaScript-heavy sites well. An llms.txt file hands them a clean summary and your priority URLs, improving the odds that models understand your brand entity and cite the right pages. It\'s one useful signal within <a href="/ai-search/llm-optimisation/">LLM optimisation</a> — not a magic bullet, but low-effort and worth doing.</p>
<h2 id="include">What to include in llms.txt</h2>
<ul><li><strong>A brand summary</strong> — your canonical entity paragraph (who you are, what you do).</li><li><strong>Key URLs with one-line descriptions</strong> — services, pillar guides, about, contact.</li><li><strong>Optional sections</strong> — grouped links (e.g. Services, Guides), and an optional <code>llms-full.txt</code> with expanded content.</li></ul>
<h2 id="create">How to create and use llms.txt</h2>
<ol><li><strong>Write a concise brand summary</strong> using your consistent entity description.</li><li><strong>List your most important URLs</strong>, grouped and each with a short description.</li><li><strong>Save it as <code>llms.txt</code></strong> in Markdown and upload it to your site root.</li><li><strong>Verify it\'s reachable</strong> at yoursite.com/llms.txt.</li><li><strong>Keep it updated</strong> as your key pages change.</li></ol>
<p>See a worked example in Alugence\'s own <a href="/llms.txt">llms.txt</a> (in this pack, <code>09-system/llms.txt</code>).</p>
<h2>Optimise your whole AI footprint</h2>
<p>Alugence\'s <a href="/ai-search/llm-optimisation/">LLM optimisation service</a> covers llms.txt and everything around it. <a href="/contact/">Book a call</a>.</p>',
		),
		array(
			'slug' => 'aeo-vs-geo', 'cpt' => 'tf_guide',
			'title' => 'AEO vs GEO vs SEO: what\'s the difference?',
			'meta_title' => 'AEO vs GEO vs SEO: What\'s the Difference?',
			'meta_desc' => 'AEO, GEO and SEO explained and compared. Understand how answer, generative and search engine optimisation differ, and when to use each.',
			'excerpt' => 'SEO optimises pages to rank as links; AEO (Answer Engine Optimisation) optimises content to be the direct answer in featured snippets and AI Overviews; GEO (Generative Engine Optimisation) optimises your brand to be cited inside generative AI answers like ChatGPT and Perplexity. They overlap heavily, and most brands need all three.',
			'faqs' => array(array('Is GEO the same as AEO?','No, but they\'re close. AEO optimises for direct-answer placements like featured snippets and AI Overviews; GEO optimises for citations inside generative answers like ChatGPT and Perplexity. They share tactics — answer-first content, structure, authority — and reinforce each other.'),array('Is SEO still relevant with AI search?','Yes. SEO fundamentals — quality content, authority, technical health — still matter and often feed the same signals AI engines use. AEO and GEO extend SEO for the AI era rather than replacing it.'),array('Should I do SEO, AEO or GEO first?','Start with solid SEO foundations, then layer AEO to win specific questions and GEO to earn AI citations. In practice much of the work overlaps, so a good strategy advances all three together. An audit helps prioritise for your situation.'),array('What\'s the difference between an answer engine and a generative engine?','An answer engine returns a single direct answer (a featured snippet, an AI Overview, a voice response). A generative engine composes an original, longer answer and may cite multiple sources (ChatGPT, Gemini, Perplexity). Google\'s AI Overviews sit at the overlap of both.'),array('Do AEO and GEO require different content?','Largely the same content serves both: clear, answer-first, well-structured, authoritative pages with FAQ sections and schema. GEO adds extra emphasis on entity consistency and off-site citations, while AEO focuses on concise, extractable answers to specific questions.'),array('Can one agency handle SEO, AEO and GEO?','Yes, and ideally one should, because the disciplines overlap so heavily that coordinating them is more efficient than splitting them. Alugence covers all three under one roof, which avoids duplicated work and conflicting priorities.'),array('Which matters most for my business?','It depends on where your customers are. If they still search Google and click, SEO and AEO dominate; if they increasingly ask AI tools for recommendations, GEO grows in importance. Most businesses are seeing a shift toward AI answers, so a balanced strategy across all three is the safe bet.'),array('Will AEO and GEO replace SEO jobs and budgets?','They\'re expanding the discipline, not replacing it. Much SEO work — content, technical health, authority — directly serves AEO and GEO. Teams and budgets are evolving to cover answer placements and AI citations alongside rankings, rather than abandoning search optimisation.')),
			'html' => '<p>Search is fragmenting into three overlapping disciplines. The good news is that they build on each other, so understanding how they differ is the key to investing sensibly rather than chasing every acronym separately. This guide defines SEO, AEO and GEO, shows where they overlap, and explains how to combine them into a single modern search strategy.</p>
<nav class="on-this-page" aria-label="On this page"><a href="#glance">The three at a glance</a> · <a href="#seo">SEO explained</a> · <a href="#aeo">AEO explained</a> · <a href="#geo">GEO explained</a> · <a href="#overlap">How they overlap</a> · <a href="#which">Which do you need?</a> · <a href="#strategy">Building one strategy</a> · <a href="#faqs">FAQs</a></nav>
<h2 id="glance">SEO, AEO and GEO at a glance</h2>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>SEO</th><th>AEO</th><th>GEO</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Full name</strong></td><td>Search Engine Optimisation</td><td>Answer Engine Optimisation</td><td>Generative Engine Optimisation</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Goal</strong></td><td>Rank pages in results</td><td>Be the direct answer</td><td>Be cited in generated answers</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Targets</strong></td><td>Google, Bing results</td><td>Featured snippets, AI Overviews, voice</td><td>ChatGPT, Gemini, Perplexity, Copilot</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Win looks like</strong></td><td>Top-ranked link</td><td>Position-zero answer</td><td>A citation in the answer</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Core levers</strong></td><td>Keywords, links, technical health</td><td>Answer-first content, FAQ, schema</td><td>Entity clarity, citations, extractability</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Measured by</strong></td><td>Rankings, clicks</td><td>Snippet/Overview capture</td><td>Mentions, share of voice</td></tr><tr><td><strong>User clicks?</strong></td><td>Usually</td><td>Sometimes</td><td>Often not</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<h2 id="seo">SEO — ranking in search results</h2>
<p>Search Engine Optimisation is the practice of improving a website so it ranks higher in traditional search results. It relies on relevant, quality content, sensible keyword targeting, backlinks from other sites, and technical health — speed, crawlability, mobile-friendliness and clean site structure.</p>
<p>SEO remains foundational for one simple reason: AI engines often draw on the same quality and authority signals that Google\'s ranking systems use. A site with strong content and authority is easier for AI to find, trust and cite. So rather than being made obsolete by AI search, good SEO becomes the bedrock that AEO and GEO build on. What\'s changing is that ranking a link is no longer the <em>only</em> goal.</p>
<h2 id="aeo">AEO — becoming the direct answer</h2>
<p>Answer Engine Optimisation structures content so an engine selects it as <em>the</em> answer — in a featured snippet, an AI Overview, or a voice response. It\'s about answering a specific question cleanly and concisely, then making that answer extractable with FAQ formatting, schema and clear structure.</p>
<p>AEO\'s defining move is <strong>answer-first writing</strong>: lead with a self-contained answer of roughly 40–60 words, then expand. Add FAQ sections, question-format headings, and lists or tables the engine can lift. The target is "position zero" — the answer shown above or instead of the ranked links. Read more in <a href="/guides/what-is-aeo/">What is AEO?</a></p>
<h2 id="geo">GEO — being cited in generative answers</h2>
<p>Generative Engine Optimisation makes your brand the source that generative AI quotes when it composes an answer. Where AEO targets a discrete answer slot, GEO targets being <em>named and cited</em> within a longer, synthesised response from ChatGPT, Gemini or Perplexity.</p>
<p>GEO leans hardest on three things: <strong>entity legibility</strong> (a consistent, machine-readable brand with schema and authoritative profiles), <strong>extractable content</strong> (clear, quotable, self-contained), and <strong>citations</strong> (mentions across trusted sources the models draw on). Read more in <a href="/guides/what-is-geo/">What is GEO?</a></p>
<h2 id="overlap">How AEO, GEO and SEO overlap</h2>
<p>Here\'s the crucial point: these disciplines share most of their work. Answer-first, well-structured, authoritative content serves AEO and GEO at once, and rests on SEO fundamentals. Schema helps all three. Topical authority helps all three. Technical accessibility helps all three.</p>
<p>The differences are at the edges. SEO adds classic ranking factors like backlinks and technical SEO. AEO adds tight, snippet-eligible answers and FAQ schema. GEO adds entity consistency and off-site citation building. You\'re not running three separate programmes so much as one strategy with three outputs — which is why treating them holistically is far more efficient than chasing each in isolation.</p>
<h2 id="which">Which do you need?</h2>
<p>For most brands, the answer is all three — but in sequence. SEO builds the foundation of quality, authority and crawlability. AEO wins the specific questions your audience asks. GEO turns that authority into citations across AI engines.</p>
<p>Where to start depends on your situation:</p>
<ul><li><strong>Weak technical or content foundations?</strong> Start with SEO fundamentals — everything else builds on them.</li><li><strong>Strong content but losing visibility to AI answers?</strong> Prioritise AEO to win answer placements.</li><li><strong>Want to be recommended when customers ask AI for options?</strong> Invest in GEO.</li></ul>
<p>If you\'re unsure, an <a href="/ai-search/ai-search-visibility/">AI search visibility audit</a> shows where you stand across all three and which lever will move the needle fastest.</p>
<h2 id="strategy">Building one search strategy across all three</h2>
<p>The most effective approach treats SEO, AEO and GEO as a single, layered strategy:</p>
<ol><li><strong>Foundations (SEO):</strong> quality content, technical health, site structure, authority.</li><li><strong>Answers (AEO):</strong> answer-first rewrites, FAQ blocks, schema, question research.</li><li><strong>Citations (GEO):</strong> entity legibility, llms.txt and crawler access, off-site citation building.</li><li><strong>Measurement:</strong> rankings <em>and</em> answer placements <em>and</em> AI citations, tracked together.</li></ol>
<p>Because the work compounds, investing in the shared foundations first pays off across all three outputs. Alugence delivers exactly this joined-up approach through its <a href="/ai-search/">AI search consultancy</a> and <a href="/ai-search/ai-seo/">AI SEO</a> practice.</p>
<h2>Get a joined-up AI search strategy</h2>
<p>Alugence covers SEO, AEO and GEO under one roof. Explore <a href="/ai-search/">our AI search consultancy</a> or <a href="/contact/">book a visibility audit</a>.</p>',
		),
		array(
			'slug' => 'ai-for-business', 'cpt' => 'tf_guide',
			'title' => 'AI for business: the complete guide',
			'meta_title' => 'AI for Business: The Complete Guide | Alugence',
			'meta_desc' => 'How to use AI in your business: where it adds value, common use cases, how to adopt it safely, and how to measure ROI. A practical guide.',
			'excerpt' => 'AI for business means applying artificial intelligence — automation, AI agents, language models and predictive analytics — to improve how an organisation operates, from cutting admin to informing decisions. Successful adoption starts with prioritising high-value use cases, getting your data ready, and rolling out with governance and training.',
			'faqs' => array(array('How can a business start using AI?','Start by assessing readiness, then pick one or two high-value use cases — usually automating admin or customer enquiries — and prove value with a small pilot before scaling. This keeps risk low and delivers a fast, visible return you can build on.'),array('What are the most common uses of AI in business?','The most common uses are automating administrative work, powering customer service with chatbots and voice agents, drafting content and documents, forecasting with predictive analytics, and searching internal knowledge. AI agents increasingly handle multi-step tasks end to end.'),array('Is AI worth it for small businesses?','Yes. Small businesses often see fast returns because AI can remove admin and provide 24/7 customer response without extra headcount, levelling the field against larger competitors. The key is starting with one or two right-sized, high-value use cases. See AI for small business.'),array('How much does it cost to adopt AI?','Costs range from low-cost tool subscriptions to larger custom builds. Many businesses start with a fixed-fee diagnostic (from around £3,500 / $4,500) and a small pilot before committing to production. See pricing and the AI ROI Calculator.'),array('What\'s the biggest reason AI projects fail?','The most common causes are poor use-case selection, weak data foundations, and neglected adoption — not the technology itself. Prioritising the right use cases, preparing data, and training people are what turn AI investment into results.')),
			'html' => '<nav class="on-this-page" aria-label="On this page"><a href="#value">Where AI adds value</a> · <a href="#usecases">Common use cases</a> · <a href="#adopt">How to adopt AI</a> · <a href="#risks">Risks &amp; governance</a> · <a href="#roi">Measuring ROI</a> · <a href="#faqs">FAQs</a></nav>
<h2 id="value">Where AI adds value in a business</h2>
<p>AI creates value in three broad ways: <strong>efficiency</strong> (automating manual work), <strong>insight</strong> (turning data into decisions), and <strong>experience</strong> (better, faster service for customers). The biggest early wins usually come from efficiency — removing repetitive admin — because the payback is fast and easy to measure.</p>
<h2 id="usecases">Common AI use cases</h2>
<ul><li><strong>Automation of admin</strong> — data entry, document processing, reporting.</li><li><strong>Customer service</strong> — <a href="/ai-consultancy/ai-chatbots/">chatbots</a> and <a href="/ai-consultancy/voice-ai/">voice agents</a> handling enquiries 24/7.</li><li><strong>Content &amp; drafting</strong> — first drafts of documents, proposals and communications.</li><li><strong>Predictive analytics</strong> — forecasting demand, churn and risk.</li><li><strong>Knowledge search</strong> — instant answers from your own documents.</li><li><strong>AI agents</strong> — multi-step tasks handled end to end (<a href="/guides/what-is-agentic-ai/">agentic AI</a>).</li></ul>
<h2 id="adopt">How to adopt AI (a practical path)</h2>
<ol><li><strong>Assess readiness</strong> — data, tools, team and momentum (<a href="/ai-consultancy/ai-readiness/">AI readiness</a>).</li><li><strong>Prioritise use cases</strong> — score on value, effort and risk (<a href="/ai-consultancy/ai-strategy/">AI strategy</a>).</li><li><strong>Prove value</strong> — run a <a href="/ai-consultancy/ai-proof-of-concept/">pilot</a> on real data.</li><li><strong>Implement &amp; integrate</strong> — build into your systems (<a href="/ai-consultancy/ai-implementation/">implementation</a>).</li><li><strong>Enable people</strong> — <a href="/ai-consultancy/ai-training/">training</a> so adoption sticks.</li><li><strong>Govern &amp; scale</strong> — controls and expansion (<a href="/ai-consultancy/ai-governance/">governance</a>).</li></ol>
<h2 id="risks">Risks and governance</h2>
<p>AI introduces risks — inaccuracy, data protection, bias, and regulatory obligations under GDPR and the EU AI Act. These are manageable with proportionate governance: classify use cases by risk, keep humans in the loop for consequential decisions, control data handling, and monitor performance. Governance is what lets you scale AI safely rather than stall on fear of it.</p>
<h2 id="roi">How to measure AI ROI</h2>
<p>Measure AI ROI by comparing the value created (hours saved, revenue gained, cost avoided) against the cost of building and running it. Efficiency projects often target payback within 6–12 months. Estimate your own potential with the <a href="/tools/ai-roi-calculator/">AI ROI Calculator</a>, and read <a href="/blog/ai-roi/">AI ROI: how to measure it</a>.</p>
<h2>Adopt AI with confidence</h2>
<p>Alugence guides businesses from strategy to working systems. Explore <a href="/ai-consultancy/">AI consultancy</a> or <a href="/contact/">book a discovery call</a>.</p>',
		),
		array(
			'slug' => 'ai-strategy-framework', 'cpt' => 'tf_guide',
			'title' => 'What is an AI strategy? A practical framework',
			'meta_title' => 'What is an AI Strategy? A Practical Framework',
			'meta_desc' => 'An AI strategy is your plan for adopting AI to hit business goals. Learn a practical framework to build one — with use cases, data and ROI.',
			'excerpt' => 'An AI strategy is a plan that defines how an organisation will use artificial intelligence to achieve its business goals — which use cases to pursue, in what order, with what data and governance, and how success is measured. A good AI strategy turns a vague ambition to "use AI" into a costed, sequenced roadmap.',
			'faqs' => array(array('What is an AI strategy?','An AI strategy is a plan for how an organisation will use AI to achieve its business goals — defining which use cases to pursue, in what order, with what data and governance, and how success is measured. It turns broad ambition into a costed, prioritised roadmap.'),array('How do you build an AI strategy?','Anchor it to business goals, discover candidate use cases, prioritise them by value, effort and risk, assess the data and governance each needs, then sequence delivery into a roadmap with owners and metrics. Starting with a readiness assessment gives you the evidence to prioritise well.'),array('What\'s the difference between an AI strategy and an AI roadmap?','An AI strategy is the overall plan — the goals, priorities and principles; an AI roadmap is the sequenced, time-bound delivery plan that flows from it. The strategy decides what and why; the roadmap decides when and in what order.'),array('How long does it take to create an AI strategy?','A focused AI strategy and roadmap typically takes two to four weeks, depending on the size of the organisation and number of use cases. A two-week AI Readiness Sprint is a common way to produce a scored use-case shortlist and initial roadmap quickly.'),array('Who should own the AI strategy?','Senior leadership should own it, because it\'s a business plan tied to goals and budget. Many organisations appoint or engage an AI leader — such as a Fractional Chief AI Officer — to own the roadmap and drive delivery.')),
			'html' => '<nav class="on-this-page" aria-label="On this page"><a href="#def">Definition</a> · <a href="#framework">The framework</a> · <a href="#mistakes">Common mistakes</a> · <a href="#faqs">FAQs</a></nav>
<h2 id="def">What an AI strategy is (and isn\'t)</h2>
<p>An AI strategy is a business plan, not a technology wish-list. It starts from your goals — grow revenue, cut cost, improve service — and works back to the AI use cases that support them. It isn\'t a list of tools to buy; it\'s a prioritised, resourced plan for where AI will create value and how you\'ll deliver it responsibly.</p>
<h2 id="framework">A 6-part AI strategy framework</h2>
<ol><li><strong>Business goals.</strong> Anchor the strategy to specific outcomes you care about.</li><li><strong>Use-case discovery.</strong> Identify candidate use cases across functions.</li><li><strong>Prioritisation.</strong> Score each on value, effort and risk to sequence them.</li><li><strong>Data &amp; foundations.</strong> Assess the data, tooling and skills each use case needs (<a href="/ai-consultancy/data-strategy/">data strategy</a>).</li><li><strong>Governance.</strong> Define risk controls, oversight and compliance (<a href="/ai-consultancy/ai-governance/">AI governance</a>).</li><li><strong>Roadmap &amp; metrics.</strong> Sequence delivery — quick wins first, foundations in parallel — with owners and success measures.</li></ol>
<p>The output is an AI roadmap leadership can fund and track. Alugence builds these through <a href="/ai-consultancy/ai-strategy/">AI strategy &amp; transformation consulting</a>, usually starting with an <a href="/ai-consultancy/ai-readiness/">AI readiness assessment</a>.</p>
<h2 id="mistakes">Common AI strategy mistakes</h2>
<ul><li><strong>Starting with technology, not goals</strong> — buying tools before defining value.</li><li><strong>Boiling the ocean</strong> — too many initiatives at once instead of sequenced quick wins.</li><li><strong>Ignoring data readiness</strong> — launching use cases the data can\'t support.</li><li><strong>Skipping governance</strong> — stalling later on risk and compliance questions.</li><li><strong>No measurement</strong> — no way to prove ROI or course-correct.</li></ul>
<h2>Build your AI strategy</h2>
<p>Alugence turns AI ambition into a costed roadmap. Explore <a href="/ai-consultancy/ai-strategy/">AI strategy consulting</a> or <a href="/contact/">book a call</a>.</p>',
