		array(
			'slug' => 'get-cited-by-ai', 'cpt' => 'tf_guide',
			'title' => 'How to get cited by AI search engines',
			'meta_title' => 'How to Get Cited by AI Search Engines | Alugence',
			'meta_desc' => 'Learn how to get your brand cited by AI search engines like ChatGPT, Perplexity and Gemini — the entity, content and authority signals that work.',
			'excerpt' => 'To get cited by AI search engines, make your brand machine-legible (consistent entity, Organization schema, sameAs profiles), give your content clean, quotable, answer-first structure that AI can extract, ensure AI crawlers can read it, and build authority through third-party citations. Then verify by tracking your mentions across engines.',
			'faqs' => array(array('How do AI search engines decide what to cite?','AI engines cite sources they can access, extract cleanly, and trust. That means crawlable, readable content; clear, self-contained answers; and authoritative, corroborated sources. Improving all three raises your chance of being cited.'),array('How do I get cited by ChatGPT specifically?','Ensure ChatGPT\'s crawlers can read your site (allow GPTBot and OAI-SearchBot, serve HTML), make your content answer-first and quotable, keep your brand entity consistent, and build citations in trusted third-party sources. Then track whether ChatGPT references you for your target prompts.'),array('Do I need backlinks to be cited by AI?','Not exactly backlinks, but third-party mentions and citations matter — they build the authority and corroboration AI models look for. A mention in a trusted publication or directory can help you get cited even without a traditional link.'),array('How can I tell if AI is citing my brand?','Run your target prompts across engines and record whether your brand is named or linked, then repeat over time to track trends. Alugence\'s AI search visibility tracking automates and benchmarks this, and adds AI referral traffic from your analytics.'),array('How long does it take to start getting cited?','It varies. Technical and content fixes can surface within weeks as engines re-crawl; authority and citation building compound over months. Low-competition topics tend to yield citations sooner, which is why AI search is such an open opportunity right now.'),array('Does content freshness affect AI citations?','Yes. Retrieval-based engines favour current sources, so regularly updated content with a visible "last updated" date is more likely to be cited than stale pages. Refreshing key pages every few months is good practice.'),array('Can any business get cited by AI, or just big brands?','Any business can. Because engines value relevance, clarity and authority on a topic — not just size — a smaller brand with genuinely expert, well-structured content can be cited for the questions it\'s authoritative on, often ahead of larger but less focused competitors.'),array('What\'s the biggest reason a brand isn\'t cited?','Usually one of three: AI crawlers can\'t read the content (JavaScript-only pages or blocked bots), the content isn\'t extractable (buried answers, poor structure), or the brand lacks third-party authority. Fixing accessibility and structure first is often the fastest route to first citations.')),
			'html' => '<p>Being cited by an AI search engine is the clearest signal that your GEO and AEO work is paying off — the model has chosen your brand as a source worth naming. This guide explains exactly what it takes to earn those citations, engine by engine, and how to verify you\'re getting them.</p>
<nav class="on-this-page" aria-label="On this page"><a href="#means">What "cited" means</a> · <a href="#why">Why citations matter</a> · <a href="#requirements">The three requirements</a> · <a href="#steps">Step-by-step</a> · <a href="#engines">Getting cited by each engine</a> · <a href="#verify">How to verify</a> · <a href="#faqs">FAQs</a></nav>
<h2 id="means">What it means to be cited by AI</h2>
<p>Being cited by an AI search engine means the model names or links to your brand as a source in the answer it generates. A citation can be an inline link (as Perplexity shows), a named brand recommendation (as ChatGPT might give), or a source listed under an AI Overview. In each case, the engine is vouching for your content by putting it in front of the user as part of its answer.</p>
<p>Citations matter because they place your brand at the exact moment of a question, lend it the engine\'s credibility, and compound: being a cited source in one context builds the authority that earns citations in others.</p>
<h2 id="why">Why AI citations matter for your business</h2>
<p>Unlike a traditional ranking, an AI citation reaches the user inside the answer they\'re already reading — no scrolling, no choosing between ten links. For high-intent questions ("what\'s the best X for Y", "who does Z in London"), being the cited answer can directly influence a purchase decision. And because engines cite only a handful of sources, a citation is a scarce, high-trust placement. Brands that earn them consistently become the default recommendation in their category — a powerful, durable advantage.</p>
<h2 id="requirements">The three requirements for AI citations</h2>
<p>AI engines cite sources that meet three conditions. Miss any one and you won\'t be cited: unreadable content can\'t be retrieved, unstructured content can\'t be extracted, and untrusted content won\'t be chosen.</p>
<h3>Accessible</h3>
<p>The content is crawlable and readable — served in clean HTML (many AI crawlers don\'t run JavaScript), with AI crawlers allowed in robots.txt and an llms.txt published. If the engine can\'t read it, nothing else matters.</p>
<h3>Extractable</h3>
<p>The content answers the question in clear, self-contained, quotable sentences. Answer-first structure, plain definitions, FAQ blocks and clean formatting all make a passage easy to lift without distortion.</p>
<h3>Trustworthy</h3>
<p>The source is authoritative, corroborated by other sources, and demonstrably expert. Named authors, credentials, cited data, and third-party mentions build the trust that makes an engine comfortable citing you.</p>
<h2 id="steps">How to get cited — step by step</h2>
<ol><li><strong>Make your entity unmistakable.</strong> Use a consistent brand name and description everywhere; add Organization schema and sameAs links to LinkedIn, Crunchbase and Companies House.</li><li><strong>Write quotable, answer-first content.</strong> Lead with the answer; use self-contained sentences that make sense out of context; define key terms plainly.</li><li><strong>Structure for machines.</strong> Question-format headings, FAQ blocks with FAQPage schema, lists and comparison tables.</li><li><strong>Open the doors.</strong> Allow AI crawlers in robots.txt; serve key content in HTML; publish an <a href="/blog/llms-txt-guide/">llms.txt</a> manifest.</li><li><strong>Build third-party authority.</strong> Earn mentions and citations in trusted publications, directories and profiles — the sources the models draw on.</li><li><strong>Show expertise (E-E-A-T).</strong> Named authors with credentials, original data, cited sources, and visible freshness.</li><li><strong>Verify and iterate.</strong> Track which engines cite you and for which prompts, then double down on what works (<a href="/ai-search/ai-search-visibility/">visibility tracking</a>).</li></ol>
<h2 id="engines">Getting cited by each AI engine</h2>
<p>The fundamentals are the same across engines, but each has nuances:</p>
<ul><li><strong>ChatGPT:</strong> allow GPTBot and OAI-SearchBot, serve content in HTML, keep your entity consistent, and build authority in sources it draws on. ChatGPT often names brands as recommendations, so being a clearly-defined, well-reviewed entity helps.</li><li><strong>Perplexity:</strong> allow PerplexityBot and lead with concise, current answers — Perplexity prominently cites sources, so clear, fresh, extractable content is well rewarded. See <a href="/guides/perplexity-optimisation/">Optimising for Perplexity</a>.</li><li><strong>Google AI Overviews:</strong> win through answer-first content, FAQ and schema, and topical authority; AI Overviews assemble from trusted, extractable sources. See <a href="/guides/google-ai-overviews/">Google AI Overviews explained</a>.</li><li><strong>Gemini and Copilot:</strong> the same accessible, extractable, authoritative signals apply; both draw on web content and favour clearly-structured, credible sources.</li></ul>
<p>Because the underlying requirements overlap, work done for one engine typically helps across all of them — which is the essence of <a href="/ai-search/llm-optimisation/">LLM optimisation</a>.</p>
<h2 id="verify">How to verify you\'re being cited</h2>
<p>You can\'t manage what you don\'t measure. Run your target prompts across the major engines and record whether your brand is named or linked, in what context, and how you compare to competitors — then repeat on a schedule to track trends. Complement this with AI referral traffic in your analytics (visits from chatgpt.com, perplexity.ai and similar). Alugence\'s <a href="/ai-search/ai-search-visibility/">AI search visibility tracking</a> automates and benchmarks this so you can prove progress and prioritise.</p>
<h2>Get cited, measurably</h2>
<p>Alugence\'s <a href="/ai-search/llm-optimisation/">LLM optimisation</a> and <a href="/ai-search/generative-engine-optimisation/">GEO</a> services get your brand cited by AI. <a href="/contact/">Book a call</a>.</p>',
