			'title' => 'What is Answer Engine Optimisation (AEO)?',
			'meta_title' => 'What is Answer Engine Optimisation (AEO)?',
			'meta_desc' => 'Answer Engine Optimisation (AEO) helps your content become the answer AI engines give. Learn what AEO is, why it matters and how to start.',
			'excerpt' => 'Answer Engine Optimisation (AEO) is the practice of structuring content so answer engines — such as Google AI Overviews, featured snippets and voice assistants — select it as the direct answer to a question. AEO focuses on giving one clear, self-contained answer that machines can extract cleanly.',
			'faqs' => array(array('What does AEO stand for?','AEO stands for Answer Engine Optimisation — the practice of structuring content so answer engines like Google AI Overviews, featured snippets and voice assistants select it as the direct answer to a question.'),array('What is the difference between AEO and SEO?','SEO optimises pages to rank in a list of links; AEO optimises content to be the single direct answer an engine gives. AEO leans on answer-first writing, FAQ formatting and schema, whereas SEO covers the broader set of ranking factors.'),array('How is AEO different from GEO?','AEO targets direct-answer placements like featured snippets and AI Overviews by answering specific questions cleanly. GEO targets citations across generative AI answers such as ChatGPT and Perplexity. They overlap heavily and reinforce each other.'),array('Does AEO help with Google AI Overviews?','Yes. AI Overviews assemble answers from sources they can extract and trust, so answer-first content, FAQ blocks, schema and topical authority all increase your chance of being used. See our AI Overviews guide.'),array('What content format works best for AEO?','Clear question-and-answer content works best: a concise answer up front, followed by supporting detail, with FAQ sections, short paragraphs, lists and comparison tables that engines can extract without ambiguity.'),array('Can small businesses benefit from AEO?','Yes. Because AEO targets specific questions, even small sites can win answers for niche or local queries where they have genuine expertise — often faster than competing for broad, high-competition rankings.'),array('How long does AEO take to work?','Some featured-snippet wins can appear within weeks of restructuring content, especially for lower-competition questions. Building the authority to win competitive answers and AI Overview placements takes longer — typically a few months.'),array('Do I need schema for AEO?','Schema isn\'t strictly required, but FAQPage and Article schema help engines understand your content\'s structure and question–answer relationships, improving your odds of selection. It\'s a low-effort, high-value part of AEO.'),array('How do I find the questions to optimise for?','Use Google\'s People Also Ask boxes, autocomplete suggestions, related searches, and question-research tools, plus your own sales and support teams, who hear real customer questions daily. Prioritise questions that are both commonly asked and commercially relevant.'),array('Is AEO worth it if AI answers reduce clicks?','Yes. Even when a user doesn\'t click, being the cited answer builds brand authority and trust, and many answer placements still drive qualified visits. The alternative — being absent from the answer entirely — is worse. Measure success on visibility and qualified traffic combined.')),
			'html' => '<p>As search shifts from links to answers, the winning position is no longer "rank number one" — it\'s "<em>be</em> the answer". Answer engine optimisation is how content earns that position. This guide explains what AEO is, why it\'s become essential, how answer engines decide what to quote, and the practical steps to make your content the answer.</p>
<nav class="on-this-page" aria-label="On this page"><a href="#means">What AEO means</a> · <a href="#why">Why AEO matters</a> · <a href="#compare">AEO vs SEO vs GEO</a> · <a href="#how">How answer engines choose</a> · <a href="#pillars">The pillars of AEO</a> · <a href="#howto">How to do AEO</a> · <a href="#measure">Measuring AEO</a> · <a href="#mistakes">Common mistakes</a> · <a href="#faqs">FAQs</a></nav>
<h2 id="means">What does answer engine optimisation mean?</h2>
<p>Answer engine optimisation means optimising your content to be chosen as <em>the answer</em> rather than one of many links. An "answer engine" is any system that returns a single direct response to a query — Google\'s AI Overviews and featured snippets, voice assistants like Siri and Alexa, and AI chat interfaces. AEO is how you win that answer slot.</p>
<p>The core idea is simple: identify the exact questions your audience asks, answer each one clearly and concisely at the top of a section, and structure it so a machine can lift it without ambiguity. It\'s less about persuading a human to click and more about giving a machine a clean, correct answer it can trust and quote.</p>
<h2 id="why">Why AEO matters</h2>
<p>Answer engines increasingly satisfy queries without a click. When Google shows an AI Overview or a featured snippet, or a voice assistant reads a single answer aloud, the source of that answer captures the attention and the credibility. Brands that win these placements become the authority in their space; brands that don\'t lose visibility even when they rank. AEO also compounds with <a href="/guides/what-is-geo/">GEO</a>: the same clear, extractable content that wins answer placements also tends to get cited in generative AI answers.</p>
<h2 id="compare">AEO vs SEO vs GEO</h2>
<p>These three disciplines overlap but aim at different targets:</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Discipline</th><th>Optimises for</th><th>Typical win</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>SEO</strong></td><td>Ranking pages as links</td><td>A top organic position</td></tr><tr><td><strong>AEO</strong></td><td>Being the direct answer</td><td>Featured snippet / AI Overview</td></tr><tr><td><strong>GEO</strong></td><td>Being cited in generative answers</td><td>A citation in ChatGPT / Perplexity</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>Most brands need all three. AEO and GEO are especially close cousins — both make content extractable and authoritative for AI — while SEO provides the quality and authority foundation both rely on. See the full comparison in <a href="/guides/aeo-vs-geo/">AEO vs GEO vs SEO</a>.</p>
<h2 id="how">How do answer engines choose an answer?</h2>
<p>Answer engines look for content that answers a specific question clearly, is easy to extract, and comes from a trusted source. In practice they favour:</p>
<ul><li>A <strong>direct answer</strong> in the first sentence of a relevant section, ideally 40–60 words for snippet eligibility.</li><li><strong>Clear structure</strong> — question-style headings, short paragraphs, lists and tables.</li><li><strong>Explicit Q&amp;A</strong> via FAQ sections and FAQPage schema, which make the question–answer relationship unmistakable.</li><li><strong>Topical authority</strong> — the site demonstrably knows the subject, with depth and credible authorship.</li><li><strong>Freshness and accuracy</strong> — current, correct information the engine can rely on.</li></ul>
<p>The engine\'s goal is to give its user a fast, correct answer. AEO simply makes your content the easiest correct answer to choose.</p>
<h2 id="pillars">The pillars of AEO</h2>
<p>Effective AEO rests on four pillars.</p>
<h3>Answer-first content</h3>
<p>Lead every section with a concise, self-contained answer, then add supporting detail. The answer must make sense on its own, because that\'s what gets extracted.</p>
<h3>Question research</h3>
<p>You can only answer questions you know are being asked. AEO starts with finding real questions — from People Also Ask, autocomplete, related searches, and your own customers and support inbox.</p>
<h3>Structure and schema</h3>
<p>FAQ blocks, FAQPage and Article schema, clear headings, lists and comparison tables all make content machine-legible and signal the question–answer relationships explicitly.</p>
<h3>Authority (E-E-A-T)</h3>
<p>Engines prefer answers from sources they trust. Named authors with credentials, cited sources, original data and genuine depth build the authority that gets your answer selected. See our approach to <a href="/about/">E-E-A-T content</a>.</p>
<h2 id="howto">How to do AEO (step by step)</h2>
<ol><li><strong>Find the questions.</strong> Use People Also Ask, autocomplete and real customer questions to build a question list.</li><li><strong>Answer first.</strong> Lead each section with a concise, self-contained answer (40–60 words for snippet eligibility).</li><li><strong>Add FAQ blocks and schema.</strong> Make the question–answer relationship explicit with FAQPage schema.</li><li><strong>Structure for extraction.</strong> Use question-format headings, short paragraphs, lists and comparison tables.</li><li><strong>Build authority.</strong> Add depth, named authors and cited sources so the engine trusts your answer.</li><li><strong>Keep it fresh.</strong> Update answers as facts change; engines favour current sources.</li><li><strong>Track placements.</strong> Monitor featured snippet and AI Overview presence, and refine.</li></ol>
<h2 id="measure">How to measure AEO</h2>
<p>Measure AEO by tracking how often your content wins featured snippets and appears in or is cited by AI Overviews for your target questions, alongside the organic traffic and impressions those queries drive. Because answer placements can reduce clicks even as they raise visibility, judge success on a blend of placement, brand exposure and qualified traffic — not clicks alone. Alugence\'s <a href="/ai-search/ai-search-visibility/">AI search visibility service</a> tracks this across engines.</p>
<h2 id="mistakes">Common AEO mistakes to avoid</h2>
<ul><li><strong>Burying the answer.</strong> Leading with preamble instead of the answer loses the snippet.</li><li><strong>Answers that need context.</strong> If the answer only makes sense after the question, it won\'t extract cleanly.</li><li><strong>No question research.</strong> Optimising for questions nobody asks wastes effort.</li><li><strong>Duplicating body content in FAQs.</strong> Repetition signals thin content; make FAQs additive.</li><li><strong>Ignoring authority.</strong> Clean structure without trust rarely wins competitive answers.</li></ul>
<h2>Win the answer</h2>
<p>Alugence\'s <a href="/ai-search/answer-engine-optimisation/">answer engine optimisation (AEO) services</a> structure your content so AI engines quote you first. <a href="/contact/">Request an AEO audit</a>.</p>',
