			'slug' => 'what-is-geo', 'cpt' => 'tf_guide',
			'title' => 'What is Generative Engine Optimisation (GEO)?',
			'meta_title' => 'What is Generative Engine Optimisation (GEO)?',
			'meta_desc' => 'Generative Engine Optimisation (GEO) is how brands get cited in AI-generated answers. Learn what GEO is, how it works and how to do it.',
			'excerpt' => 'Generative Engine Optimisation (GEO) is the practice of optimising a brand and its content to be cited in the answers produced by generative AI engines such as ChatGPT, Google Gemini, Perplexity and Google AI Overviews. Where traditional SEO aims to rank links, GEO aims to make your brand the source the AI quotes.',
			'faqs' => array(array('What does GEO stand for?','GEO stands for Generative Engine Optimisation — the practice of optimising a brand and its content to be cited in answers generated by AI engines like ChatGPT, Gemini and Perplexity.'),array('Is GEO the same as SEO?','No, but they overlap. SEO optimises pages to rank as links; GEO optimises your brand to be cited inside a generated answer. GEO adds entity clarity, answer-first content and citation building on top of SEO fundamentals, rather than replacing them.'),array('Is GEO the same as AI SEO?','The terms are often used loosely. "AI SEO" tends to be a broad umbrella for optimising in the age of AI search (and using AI tools for SEO), while GEO specifically means optimising to be cited in generative answers. GEO sits within the wider AI SEO discipline.'),array('How do you measure GEO success?','GEO success is measured by how often your brand is mentioned and cited across AI engines for your target prompts, and your share of voice versus competitors, tracked over time. AI referral traffic in analytics is a useful downstream signal.'),array('How long does GEO take to work?','Some improvements appear within weeks as engines re-crawl and update, but building durable citation strength typically takes a few months of sustained work. Because many AI-search terms are still low-competition, results can come faster than in mature SEO.'),array('Can I do GEO myself?','You can start — standardising your brand entity, adding schema, and writing answer-first content are all achievable in-house. Advanced GEO (citation building, cross-engine tracking, technical access) is where a specialist like Alugence adds the most value.'),array('Which engines does GEO target?','GEO targets generative AI engines including ChatGPT, Google Gemini, Google AI Overviews, Perplexity and Microsoft Copilot — any system that composes an answer and cites sources.'),array('Do backlinks help with GEO?','Indirectly, yes. Links and mentions from trusted sites build the authority and corroboration generative models draw on, and often correlate with being cited. But entity clarity and extractable content matter just as much — GEO is about being *understood and trusted*, not just linked.'),array('Does GEO replace SEO?','No. GEO extends SEO for the AI era. Strong SEO foundations — quality content, authority, technical health — underpin GEO, and you should keep investing in both. The smart approach is one modern search strategy that earns rankings *and* AI citations.'),array('What content works best for GEO?','Answer-first, well-structured, authoritative content: clear definitions, self-contained sentences, question-format headings, FAQ sections, and comparison tables. Content that a model can lift and quote without losing meaning performs best.')),
			'html' => '<p>Generative AI has changed how people find information. Instead of scanning a page of blue links, users increasingly read a single synthesised answer — and that answer names only a handful of sources. GEO is how you become one of them. This guide explains what generative engine optimisation is, why it matters, how generative engines choose what to cite, and the practical steps to grow your presence in AI answers.</p>
<nav class="on-this-page" aria-label="On this page"><a href="#what-geo-means">What GEO means</a> · <a href="#why">Why GEO matters now</a> · <a href="#geo-vs-seo">GEO vs SEO</a> · <a href="#engines">How generative engines work</a> · <a href="#levers">The three levers of GEO</a> · <a href="#how-to-do-geo">How to do GEO</a> · <a href="#measure">Measuring GEO</a> · <a href="#mistakes">Common mistakes</a> · <a href="#geo-aeo">GEO and AEO</a> · <a href="#faqs">FAQs</a></nav>
<h2 id="what-geo-means">What does generative engine optimisation mean?</h2>
<p>Generative engine optimisation means shaping your brand\'s online presence so generative AI models understand it, trust it, and cite it when they answer relevant questions. A "generative engine" is any AI system that composes an original answer rather than returning a list of links — ChatGPT, Gemini, Perplexity, Copilot and Google\'s AI Overviews all qualify.</p>
<p>The term GEO emerged as marketers recognised that AI answers were becoming a distinct discovery channel with its own rules. It\'s sometimes used interchangeably with "AI SEO" or "LLM optimisation", but GEO specifically describes optimising for <em>generative</em> answers — being quoted inside the response the model writes. See how it fits alongside related disciplines in <a href="/guides/aeo-vs-geo/">AEO vs GEO vs SEO</a>.</p>
<h2 id="why">Why GEO matters now</h2>
<p>Three forces make GEO urgent. First, <strong>behaviour is shifting</strong>: more people ask AI engines directly rather than searching and clicking, so the answer — not the link — is where attention lands. Second, <strong>AI answers cite few sources</strong>: being one of the three or four brands an engine names is far more exclusive than ranking on page one. Third, <strong>the field is still open</strong>: many AI-search terms are low-competition today, so brands that move early can build authority that\'s hard to displace later.</p>
<p>If AI engines don\'t know, trust and cite your brand, you\'re invisible at the exact moment of decision — even if you rank well in classic search. GEO closes that gap.</p>
<h2 id="geo-vs-seo">GEO vs SEO: what\'s the difference?</h2>
<p>GEO and SEO share DNA — both reward quality, structure and authority — but they optimise for different outputs.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>SEO</th><th>GEO</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Goal</strong></td><td>Rank a page in a list of links</td><td>Be cited inside a generated answer</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Unit of success</strong></td><td>Position on the results page</td><td>Mention / citation in the answer</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Key levers</strong></td><td>Keywords, backlinks, technical SEO</td><td>Entity clarity, extractable content, citations, schema</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Measured by</strong></td><td>Rankings, clicks</td><td>Share of voice, citations across AI engines</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Click behaviour</strong></td><td>User clicks through to your site</td><td>User may never click — the answer is consumed in place</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>The practical takeaway: GEO doesn\'t replace SEO, it extends it. Many of the same foundations apply — quality content, technical health, authority — but GEO adds entity consistency, answer-first writing and citation building aimed squarely at AI models. A brand strong in SEO has a head start in GEO, but the two are not the same, and strong rankings don\'t guarantee AI citations.</p>
<h2 id="engines">How generative engines choose what to cite</h2>
<p>To do GEO well, it helps to understand how generative engines actually produce answers. Most combine two steps: <strong>retrieval</strong> (finding relevant, current information) and <strong>generation</strong> (using a language model to compose an answer from it). This "retrieval-augmented generation" is why engines can cite up-to-date sources rather than only their training data.</p>
<p>At each stage, the engine makes choices that GEO can influence:</p>
<ul><li>It <strong>retrieves</strong> content it can access and parse — so your content must be crawlable and readable.</li><li>It <strong>selects</strong> passages that clearly and directly answer the query — so your content must be extractable.</li><li>It <strong>prefers</strong> sources it can identify and trust — so your brand entity and authority must be strong.</li><li>It <strong>cites</strong> a small number of sources — so the bar for inclusion is high.</li></ul>
<p>Understanding this makes GEO tactics feel less like guesswork and more like removing the specific obstacles between your content and a citation.</p>
<h2 id="levers">The three levers of GEO</h2>
<p>GEO works on three connected levers.</p>
<h3>1. Entity legibility (make the brand machine-readable)</h3>
<p>Generative models build an understanding of your brand as an <em>entity</em>. If your name, description and details are consistent everywhere, the model forms a clear, confident picture and is more likely to cite you accurately. If they\'re inconsistent, the entity is fuzzy and the model hesitates. Entity legibility means: a consistent brand string everywhere, Organization schema, and <code>sameAs</code> links to authoritative profiles (LinkedIn, Crunchbase, Companies House). Build a <a href="/glossary/">glossary</a> of the terms you want to own, too.</p>
<h3>2. Extractable content (make it easy to quote)</h3>
<p>Models cite content they can lift cleanly. That means answer-first writing (lead with the answer), self-contained sentences that make sense out of context, question-format headings, FAQ sections, and structured formats like lists and comparison tables. Content buried in preamble or dependent on surrounding context is hard to extract and rarely cited.</p>
<h3>3. Authority and citations (make it trustworthy)</h3>
<p>Models favour sources corroborated elsewhere. Mentions and citations across trusted publications, directories and profiles build the authority that gets you referenced — the AI-era descendant of link building, focused on being <em>talked about</em> by credible sources. Demonstrable expertise (named authors, credentials, original data) reinforces this.</p>
<h2 id="how-to-do-geo">How to do GEO (step by step)</h2>
<ol><li><strong>Audit your current citations.</strong> Find out where AI engines already mention you and where competitors win instead (<a href="/ai-search/ai-search-visibility/">AI search visibility</a>).</li><li><strong>Fix entity legibility.</strong> Standardise your brand name everywhere, add Organization schema and <code>sameAs</code> profiles.</li><li><strong>Make content answer-first.</strong> Open each page and section with a concise, quotable answer; add FAQ blocks.</li><li><strong>Structure for extraction.</strong> Use clear question-format headings, comparison tables and numbered steps.</li><li><strong>Enable technical access.</strong> Server-side render key content (many AI crawlers don\'t run JavaScript), allow AI crawlers in robots.txt, and publish an <a href="/blog/llms-txt-guide/">llms.txt</a>.</li><li><strong>Build citations.</strong> Earn mentions in the trusted sources models draw on — publications, directories, profiles.</li><li><strong>Keep content fresh.</strong> Update key pages regularly; retrieval systems favour current sources.</li><li><strong>Measure and iterate.</strong> Track citations and share of voice over time, and double down on what moves.</li></ol>
<p>For the deeper method behind steps 2–6, read <a href="/guides/get-cited-by-ai/">how to get cited by AI search engines</a>.</p>
<h2 id="measure">How to measure GEO success</h2>
<p>GEO success is measured by how often your brand is mentioned and cited across AI engines for your target prompts, and your <strong>share of voice</strong> versus competitors, tracked over time. This is the AI-era equivalent of rank tracking. A practical measurement approach runs your customers\' real prompts across engines, records where and how your brand appears, and repeats on a schedule to show trends. You should also track <strong>AI referral traffic</strong> in analytics — visits from chatgpt.com, perplexity.ai and similar — as a downstream signal. Alugence\'s <a href="/ai-search/ai-search-visibility/">AI search visibility tracking</a> automates and benchmarks this.</p>
<h2 id="mistakes">Common GEO mistakes to avoid</h2>
<ul><li><strong>Treating GEO as keyword stuffing.</strong> Generative engines reward clarity and authority, not keyword density.</li><li><strong>Ignoring the entity.</strong> Inconsistent brand names and missing schema leave models unsure who you are.</li><li><strong>JavaScript-only content.</strong> If key content isn\'t in the initial HTML, many AI crawlers never see it.</li><li><strong>Thin, unstructured pages.</strong> Content that can\'t be extracted cleanly won\'t be cited.</li><li><strong>No measurement.</strong> Without tracking citations and share of voice, you can\'t tell what\'s working.</li><li><strong>Expecting instant results.</strong> Some wins come in weeks; durable authority takes months.</li></ul>
<h2 id="geo-aeo">GEO and AEO: two sides of AI search</h2>
<p>GEO is closely related to <a href="/guides/what-is-aeo/">answer engine optimisation (AEO)</a>. AEO focuses on winning direct-answer placements — featured snippets and AI Overviews — by answering specific questions cleanly. GEO focuses on being cited across generative answers like ChatGPT and Perplexity. They share most tactics (answer-first content, structure, schema, authority) and reinforce each other, which is why most brands pursue both as one AI search strategy. Alugence delivers both through its <a href="/ai-search/">AI search consultancy</a>.</p>
<h2>Get cited in AI answers</h2>
<p>Alugence\'s <a href="/ai-search/generative-engine-optimisation/">generative engine optimisation (GEO) services</a> make your brand the answer AI gives. <a href="/contact/">Request a GEO audit</a> to see where you stand.</p>',
		),
	);
}

